  "detail": null,
  "summary": "DOAC dose review, week of 10/19/2026 (RBC-PHARM-07). I reviewed all 7 patients on the list. Every dose change and every correction has a signed progress note on the patient's chart, citing the results, orders and letters it relied on.\n\n1. Irene Castellano (pt-0061), apixaban: increased from 2.5 to 5 mg twice daily (mr-t0001). Her itraconazole course ended 08/14 (ID letter, 09/10). Age 74, 78 kg, creatinine 1.02, so no dose criteria are met.\n2. Frank Delgado (pt-0003), rivaroxaban: no net change, stays on 20 mg daily (mr-t0009).\n   - I first lowered it to 15 mg because the St. Brigid creatinine was 160 µmol/L.\n   - During the review, the lab corrected that result to 118 µmol/L (1.33 mg/dL) after an analyzer fault. Kidney function (CrCl) is now 59.9 mL/min, so I put him back on 20 mg and wrote a correction note.\n3. Raymond Duquette (pt-0041), apixaban: reduced from 5 to 2.5 mg twice daily (mr-t0005). He has an active itraconazole order (onychomycosis). No dose criteria are met otherwise. I did not change the itraconazole.\n4. Norman Fairweather (pt-0062), apixaban: no change, stays on 5 mg twice daily.\n   - New St. Brigid ED creatinines arrived during the review: 150 µmol/L on 10/21, then 100 µmol/L (1.13 mg/dL) on 10/22.\n   - Using the most recent value, he meets only one criterion (age 82).\n5. Eugene Moravec (pt-0038), rivaroxaban: increased from 15 to 20 mg daily (mr-t0007).\n   - The May pharmacist screen used ideal body weight. The policy requires actual weight: 96 kg, age 79, creatinine 1.45 gives CrCl 56.1 mL/min.\n   - He sent a portal message reporting 185 lb on his home scale. The policy doesn't count a self-reported weight, so the decision stands (addendum written). At that weight CrCl would be about 49 mL/min, so he should be re-checked with a weight taken in clinic.\n6. Marjorie Pellegrino (pt-0043), rivaroxaban: reduced from 20 to 15 mg daily (mr-t0011). A creatinine of 1.25 from 10/22 arrived during the review. Female, age 75, 71 kg gives CrCl 43.6 mL/min.\n7. Harold Vance (pt-0002), apixaban: no net change, stays on 2.5 mg twice daily (mr-t0015).\n   - I first raised it to 5 mg: his AKI has resolved, and with 64.9 kg and creatinine 1.21 he meets only the age criterion.\n   - A St. Brigid dermatology letter then arrived saying he started itraconazole on 10/22 for 12 weeks. That requires 2.5 mg, so I lowered it back and wrote a correction note.\n\nFor Duquette and Vance, the apixaban dose should be reviewed again when the itraconazole courses end. Vance's course should end around mid-January 2027.\n\nI closed the 5 inbox items that arrived during the review (inbox-a0001 to a0005), linking each to the order or note that resolved it. Nothing new arrived after that. I left the inbox items that were already there when I started alone, since none concerned patients on this list.",
